<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Michoacán</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Michoacán is a state of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, stretching from the Pacific coast to the volcanic highlands, with its capital at Morelia. It was the heartland of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/purepecha-empire">Purépecha</a> state, which resisted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztecs</a> and submitted to the Spanish between 1522 and 1530. In the colonial period it became a bishopric under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasco-de-quiroga">Vasco de Quiroga</a>, and it was a key region in the war of independence.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/michoacan.jpg" alt="Michoacán" width="320" height="225" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mauricio C. · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cerro_Patamban.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Michoacán lies in west-central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, in the extreme south-west of the central highlands, with a coastline of 217 km on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pacific-ocean">Pacific Ocean</a>. It borders Colima and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jalisco">Jalisco</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guanajuato">Guanajuato</a>, Querétaro, the State of México and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrero">Guerrero</a>. Its name comes from Nahuatl Michhuahcan, 'place of the fishermen', which refers to those who fish on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-patzcuaro">Lake Pátzcuaro</a>. Vegetation changes with altitude, from conifer forests above 2,600 metres to tropical forests lower down, and the state holds the main wintering ground of the monarch butterflies, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/purepecha-empire">Purépecha</a> take to be the spirits of the dead.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The first settlers were hunter-gatherers of the Palaeoindian period, known from fluted projectile points found with the bones of mammoths and bison. The Preclassic population lived in agricultural villages at El Opeño in the west and in the Balsas basin in the south, and for a long time the region held various local cultures. By about 900 CE populations had moved to defensible places in the Cuitzeo and Zacapu basins and along the Lerma and Balsas rivers.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/purepecha-empire">Purépecha</a>, said to descend from late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chichimeca">Chichimeca</a> arrivals from the north, began their state in the thirteenth century at Uayameo, today Santa Fé de la Laguna, and by the fifteenth century dominated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-patzcuaro">Lake Pátzcuaro</a> area. Between 1401 and 1450 they conquered neighbouring peoples, and by the late fifteenth century their state rivalled that of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztecs</a> and reached into parts of Colima, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nayarit">Nayarit</a>, Querétaro, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guanajuato">Guanajuato</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrero">Guerrero</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jalisco">Jalisco</a>. The Aztecs attacked and were repelled, and the Purépecha later refused them aid against the Spanish. They were also skilled in metallurgy, especially in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a>.</p><p>The ruler Zuanga died of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">smallpox</a> before any Spaniard arrived, and Tanganxoan II succeeded him. The first Spaniard to reach the area was Cristóbal de Olid. After the Spanish destroyed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a>, Tanganxoan agreed to submit, on a promise that he would remain ruler, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuno-de-guzman">Nuño de Guzmán</a> went back on the agreement and killed him in 1530. The empire became a separate province that grew smaller over the colonial period. The state and its people played a major part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexican-war-of-independence">Mexican War of Independence</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Michoacán is one of the 31 states of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, divided into 113 municipalities, with its capital at Morelia, formerly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valladolid">Valladolid</a>, named after the independence hero <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-maria-morelos">José María Morelos</a>, who was born there. Morelia's colonial centre, pink stone cathedral and aqueduct were declared a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 1991. Many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/purepecha-empire">Purépecha</a> still live in the state, and its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/day-of-the-dead">Day of the Dead</a> celebrations at Janitzio and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patzcuaro">Pátzcuaro</a> are among the best known in Mexico.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Michoacán” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Conquistadors spread out to subdue Oaxaca, <b>Michoacán</b>, Guatemala and the Gulf coast, while Nuño de Guzmán’s brutal campaigns in the 1530s created the Kingdom of New Galicia in the west.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-spain">New Spain · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Purépecha Empire, called the Tarascan state by the Spaniards, grew up around Lake Pátzcuaro in the volcanic highlands of <b>Michoacán</b>, in western Mexico.</blockquote><a href="/empire/purepecha-empire">Purépecha Empire · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
